### Character quality gate

The final character must have credible human proportions, proper silhouette, materials/textures, UVs, skeleton, skin weights, and suitable animation. It must not be a stack of boxes or a capsule-only placeholder presented as final.

Validate idle, walk, run, turning, stop/start, ground contact, model orientation, scale, and camera behavior. Retarget compatible licensed animations if needed and test in Blender and in the browser.

### Vehicle quality gate

The first vehicle must be detailed enough to read as an actual car at gameplay distance. Check body proportions, wheels, glass, lights/materials, scale, pivots, steering, and collision setup. Separate render meshes from simple collision proxies. Test the model and physics in the browser.

### City/environment quality gate

The district must not be a flat plane populated by identical boxes. Use varied licensed environment assets, coherent building placements, sidewalks, curbs, road markings, signs, street props, vegetation, and a few high-quality landmark buildings.

Use procedural tools for placement, repeatable variations, road geometry, and missing low-priority elements—not as a substitute for required model quality.

### Browser asset pipeline

Prefer GLB/glTF when suitable. Validate model scale, materials, textures, animation clips, compression support, and external dependencies. Optimize assets only if visual quality and animations remain correct.

Create a browser-based asset validation gallery before integrating a large quantity of assets into the main gameplay scene.

