## 12. PHASE 5 — GAMEPLAY AND PHYSICS

### Character
Implement third-person movement, turning, stopping, animation transitions, stable camera follow, camera collision, ground detection, and building/road collision. Validate input mapping and ensure controls are not reversed.

### Vehicle
Implement acceleration, braking, reverse, steering, wheel synchronization, collision, entry/exit, and explicit player/vehicle state transitions. Entry/exit must use valid positions and must not teleport the character into a wall or vehicle mesh. Avoid duplicated physics updates and unstable force application.

### Physics and interactions
Use Rapier for appropriate rigid bodies, colliders, raycasts, contact events, and sensors. Keep render meshes and collision geometry separate. Use correct collision groups and nearest-hit logic. Shooting/interaction rays must respect blocking geometry when required.

Test ground contact, wall collisions, overlap cases, impacts, speed-related tunnelling, and vehicle recovery.

### Traffic and pedestrians
Begin with a bounded number of NPCs and vehicles. Use road graphs and navigation paths. Prevent movement through buildings, random direction reversals, permanent deadlocks, duplicated updates, and uncontrolled entity growth. Scale up only after profiling.

### First mission
Implement one complete, playable mission with:
- Start trigger and instructions.
- Clear objectives.
- A route or destination inside the district.
- At least one meaningful player/vehicle interaction.
- Completion and failure conditions.
- Clear user feedback.
- Restart/retry behavior.
- Reproducible acceptance tests where practical.

A mission screen that cannot be completed in actual gameplay is not complete.

## 13. PHASE 6 — BROWSER PERFORMANCE

The core game must render on the player's device. Do not depend on cloud GPU streaming to meet the initial requirements.

Use and measure:
- Frustum and distance culling.
- LOD for buildings, vehicles, characters, and props.
- Instancing and shared materials.
- Compressed geometry and textures.
- District/region streaming.
- Bounded shadow rendering and selective expensive effects.
- Asynchronous asset loading and loading feedback.
- Resource disposal for geometries, textures, materials, listeners and physics bodies.
- Stable physics timing and bounded AI updates.
- Low/medium/high quality presets.

Set explicit performance budgets after inspecting the target environment and asset sizes. Track compressed download size, decoded texture memory, geometry, draw calls, visible entities, time to interactive, frame times, and memory behavior.

Target 60 FPS on a documented suitable desktop configuration where feasible. Treat this as a testable goal, not a promise. Measure frame-time percentiles and test with real gameplay content. If the target fails, profile, optimize, reduce scope, or document the limitation.

Do not claim compatibility with every browser or computer. Test and document a real compatibility matrix.

